= Semantic Web =

== Overview ==
The '''Semantic Web''' is a family of Web standards and architectural ideas for publishing, linking and interpreting machine-readable data using global identifiers, shared vocabularies and explicit [[Semantics|semantics]].

Its central technologies include:
* [[Resource Description Framework|RDF]];
* RDF Schema;
* [[Web Ontology Language|OWL]];
* SPARQL;
* SKOS;
* related Linked Data practices.

The goal is not to make computers understand the Web exactly as humans do. It is to make more meaning explicit enough for interoperable software to process.

== Web of data ==
Traditional Web pages primarily link documents.

Semantic Web technologies can link data about things.

Example:

<pre>
resource: Mars
type: Planet
orbits: Sun
</pre>

If identifiers and vocabularies are shared, independently published data can connect across sources.

== Global identifiers ==
Semantic Web systems commonly use IRIs to identify:
* entities;
* concepts;
* properties;
* classes;
* vocabularies.

Global identifiers reduce name collisions.

They do not automatically solve [[Identity|identity]].

Two IRIs may refer to the same thing, and one poorly governed IRI may be ambiguous.

== RDF ==
[[Resource Description Framework|RDF]] provides the basic graph data model.

Information is expressed as triples:

<pre>
subject — predicate — object
</pre>

Triples combine into graphs and datasets.

RDF itself supplies a minimal semantic foundation.

== RDF Schema ==
RDF Schema adds basic vocabulary modeling:
* classes;
* subclass relationships;
* properties;
* subproperties;
* domain;
* range.

It supports semantic inference beyond raw triples.

== OWL ==
[[Web Ontology Language|OWL]] adds richer ontology semantics.

It can represent:
* class expressions;
* equivalence;
* disjointness;
* identity;
* property characteristics;
* cardinality restrictions.

OWL enables stronger automated reasoning at greater complexity.

== SPARQL ==
SPARQL is a query language/protocol family for RDF data.

A graph pattern can retrieve resources whose triples match a query.

SPARQL queries do not necessarily imply full OWL reasoning; the active entailment regime matters.

== Linked Data ==
'''Linked Data''' is a related publishing practice emphasizing Web identifiers and links among datasets.

A Linked Data system can use RDF without using rich OWL ontologies.

Linked Data is therefore part of the Semantic Web ecosystem, not synonymous with the entire Semantic Web.

== SKOS ==
SKOS represents lighter-weight knowledge organization systems such as:
* thesauri;
* taxonomies;
* subject headings.

It deliberately avoids requiring the full formal commitments of OWL.

This lets publishers represent concept schemes without converting every broader/narrower relation into logical subclasshood.

== Open-world assumption ==
Semantic Web ontology systems often operate under an open-world assumption.

If a graph does not say that Alice has a phone number, the system does not normally infer that she has none.

This differs from closed-world database applications where absence can sometimes be treated as false or invalid.

== Decentralization ==
The Semantic Web is designed for decentralized publication.

Different organizations can define vocabularies and publish graphs independently.

This creates both power and difficulty:
* global linking;
* vocabulary reuse;
* conflicting claims;
* identity alignment;
* versioning;
* trust/provenance.

No central database resolves every disagreement.

== Trust and provenance ==
Machine-readable semantics does not guarantee trustworthy content.

Semantic Web data still needs:
* [[Provenance|provenance]];
* source evaluation;
* versioning;
* signatures/attestations where appropriate;
* conflict handling.

A logically valid inference from false premises remains untrustworthy.

== Semantic Web and AI ==
Semantic Web technologies can support AI by providing explicit symbolic structure.

Modern systems can combine:
* RDF/OWL knowledge;
* search;
* machine learning;
* embeddings;
* language models.

The symbolic layer provides explicit semantics; learned models provide statistical generalization.

They should not be conflated.

== Historical vision ==
The term became widely known through Tim Berners-Lee, James Hendler and Ora Lassila's 2001 Scientific American article describing a Web where machine agents could combine structured information from multiple sources.

The standards evolved considerably after that vision.

Current explanations should focus on actual W3C technologies rather than assuming the original illustrative scenarios are the present architecture.

== Epistemic notes ==
"Semantic Web" does not mean that every Web resource has formal semantics or that software achieves human-level understanding. It is an infrastructure for making selected meanings machine-processable.

